<LegalPage title="Editorial policy" description="How campaign records are captured, reviewed, updated, and corrected." path="/editorial-policy/" kicker="EDITORIAL POLICY / HOW RECORDS WORK">
  <p>We use public campaign listings as leads, then store a dated snapshot of factual fields. The source name, chain, requirement labels, reward wording, and source URL are kept separate from our original explanatory copy.</p>
  <h2>Review states</h2>
  <ul><li><strong>Source snapshot:</strong> required fields are present, but readers should still verify the official campaign page.</li><li><strong>Needs review:</strong> a field is missing or ambiguous and the page is not treated as a complete guide.</li><li><strong>Unverified:</strong> the record needs evidence before it should be used for a decision.</li></ul>
  <h2>Updates and corrections</h2>
  <p>Every record shows a capture date. When a source changes, we update the local record or mark it for review. Send corrections with the exact page URL and supporting source through the <a class="text-link" href="/corrections/">corrections page</a>.</p>
  <h2>Sponsored content</h2>
  <p>Any paid placement must be visibly labelled Sponsored. Payment does not remove risk notes or turn an unverified campaign into a recommendation. Paid and affiliate links receive appropriate sponsored and nofollow attributes.</p>
</LegalPage>
